<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan III Sobieski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1629">1629</a>–<a href="/years/1696">1696</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan III Sobieski was King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in the late seventeenth century and an accomplished military commander. In 1683 he led the relief army that defeated the Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. The victory halted Ottoman expansion into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and is remembered as a high point of the Commonwealth's military history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski.jpg" alt="Jan III Sobieski" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Daniel Schultz / Possibly Andreas Stech · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schultz_John_III_Sobieski.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive turn came in 1683, when a relief army led by <b>John III Sobieski</b> of Poland broke the second Ottoman siege of Vienna at the Battle of Kahlenberg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…second siege of Vienna in September 1683, when a relief army of imperial German and Polish troops under <b>Jan III Sobieski</b> routed the forces of Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa Pasha, marked the end of Ottoman expansion in Europe and opened a long era of territorial retreat that the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Militarily, the Commonwealth played a decisive role in the relief of Vienna in 1683, where <b>Jan III Sobieski</b> led the great cavalry charge that broke Kara Mustafa’s besieging army, and it joined the Holy League that went on to roll back Ottoman power in central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Olesko, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Wilanów Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.96,24.89&amp;year=1672" aria-label="Olesko on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5121 425 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jan III Sobieski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5571 695h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5571 695h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.96,24.89&amp;year=1672">What was here in 1672? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
